WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.806
Picture a small theatre company putting on a play.

2
00:00:05.806 --> 00:00:09.161
A new prop, say a crown, doesn't just appear on stage.

3
00:00:09.161 --> 00:00:10.341
Someone fetches it.

4
00:00:10.341 --> 00:00:14.566
The crew checks it, and gives it a labelled spot on the props table.

5
00:00:14.566 --> 00:00:17.859
Only at the dress rehearsal does an actor pick it up.

6
00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:21.532
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:21.532 --> 00:00:26.161
When my code mentions a class, its start-up code runs right then, doesn't it?

8
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.404
Let's start from code you know.

9
00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:31.575
A class can have start-up code.

10
00:00:31.575 --> 00:00:33.256
In Java, a static block.

11
00:00:33.256 --> 00:00:36.058
In Kotlin, an object with an init block.

12
00:00:36.058 --> 00:00:41.240
The JVM, the program that runs your compiled code, runs it once per class.

13
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.604
Here's a tiny class, Limits.

14
00:00:43.604 --> 00:00:45.360
MAX_CAST is an int, 12.

15
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.422
CAP is an Integer, also 12.

16
00:00:47.422 --> 00:00:49.941
And the static block prints init.

17
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:54.542
Now another class prints Limits.MAX_CAST, and then Limits.CAP.

18
00:00:54.542 --> 00:00:56.095
When does init appear?

19
00:00:56.095 --> 00:00:59.624
Before the first value, between the two, or never?

20
00:00:59.624 --> 00:01:00.753
Pause and guess.

21
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:05.311
Hold on to that guess.

22
00:01:05.311 --> 00:01:08.324
Bringing in a class sounds like one event.

23
00:01:08.324 --> 00:01:10.260
Inside the JVM, it's three.

24
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:12.304
Back to the theatre.

25
00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:14.891
A new prop goes through three steps.

26
00:01:14.891 --> 00:01:16.759
First, someone fetches it.

27
00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:17.837
That's loading.

28
00:01:17.837 --> 00:01:25.599
The JVM finds the class's bytes, in a folder or a jar, and turns them into a Class object, with defineClass.

29
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.199
Second, before any scene uses it, the crew gets it ready.

30
00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:30.451
They check it's safe.

31
00:01:30.451 --> 00:01:33.790
They give it an empty, labelled spot on the props table.

32
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.307
And they fill in the cue sheet that says where it's needed.

33
00:01:37.307 --> 00:01:40.169
That's linking, and it has the same three parts.

34
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.137
Checking is verification.

35
00:01:42.137 --> 00:01:50.197
The JVM checks that the bytecode, the instructions inside, is type-safe: no instruction treats a number as a string.

36
00:01:50.197 --> 00:01:52.281
The empty spot is preparation.

37
00:01:52.281 --> 00:01:57.701
The static fields are created, and set to their defaults: zero, false or null.

38
00:01:57.701 --> 00:01:59.924
And the cue sheet is resolution.

39
00:01:59.924 --> 00:02:04.163
Names, like method fire in class Lights, become direct links.

40
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:09.490
Third comes the dress rehearsal, the only step that puts actors on stage.

41
00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.034
That's initialization.

42
00:02:11.034 --> 00:02:15.315
The JVM runs the class's static start-up code, top to bottom.

43
00:02:15.315 --> 00:02:20.649
javac collects it all into one method, named &lt;clinit&gt;, short for class init.

44
00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:23.266
So two of the three steps are crew work.

45
00:02:23.266 --> 00:02:27.466
Nobody claps for the crew, and none of your code runs while they work.

46
00:02:27.466 --> 00:02:29.505
Only the rehearsal runs your code.

47
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:34.491
So loading a class and running its start-up code are separate events.

48
00:02:34.491 --> 00:02:39.731
HotSpot, the JVM inside your JDK, does each step as late as it's allowed to.

49
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:43.477
And here's where the theatre picture breaks.

50
00:02:43.477 --> 00:02:46.709
A real crew checks the whole prop before the show.

51
00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:53.432
The JVM may fill in the cue sheet lazily, one name at a time, and never at all for code that never runs.

52
00:02:53.633 --> 00:02:55.004
The lesson tried it.

53
00:02:55.004 --> 00:02:59.186
A class that used a deleted Helper class loaded and ran fine.

54
00:02:59.186 --> 00:03:04.533
It failed with NoClassDefFoundError only when the branch that used Helper ran.

55
00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:07.653
So which uses start the rehearsal?

56
00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:09.536
Creating an object with new.

57
00:03:09.536 --> 00:03:11.150
Calling a static method.

58
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:12.832
Assigning a static field.

59
00:03:12.832 --> 00:03:15.858
Reading a static field that isn't a constant.

60
00:03:15.858 --> 00:03:18.952
Reflection, such as Class.forName with a name.

61
00:03:18.952 --> 00:03:22.651
And a subclass always initializes its superclass first.

62
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:24.058
And these don't.

63
00:03:24.058 --> 00:03:25.472
Reading a constant.

64
00:03:25.472 --> 00:03:27.036
Writing Limits.class.

65
00:03:27.036 --> 00:03:29.418
Making an empty array of Limits.

66
00:03:29.418 --> 00:03:33.289
Or Class.forName, with false as its second argument.

67
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.778
Now, back to your guess.

68
00:03:35.778 --> 00:03:39.919
The lesson's InitPuzzle prints a line whenever a &lt;clinit&gt; runs.

69
00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.431
Here's its real output.

70
00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:45.420
Step 3 reads Limits.MAX_CAST, and nothing prints.

71
00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.665
Step 4 reads Limits.CAP, and there it is: Limits.&lt;clinit&gt;.

72
00:03:49.665 --> 00:03:52.520
So init appears between the two values.

73
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:54.874
The rest agrees with the list.

74
00:03:54.874 --> 00:03:59.156
Step 1 wakes only Parent, the class that declares the field.

75
00:03:59.156 --> 00:04:00.726
new Child wakes Child.

76
00:04:00.726 --> 00:04:04.152
The array, Lazy.class and forName print nothing.

77
00:04:04.152 --> 00:04:06.079
Only Lazy.touch wakes Lazy.

78
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.909
So why is MAX_CAST different?

79
00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:15.424
It's a constant variable: a static final primitive or String whose value the compiler can work out.

80
00:04:15.424 --> 00:04:19.242
javac copied the 12 straight into the caller's class file.

81
00:04:19.242 --> 00:04:22.664
At run time, the caller never touches Limits at all.

82
00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:24.212
CAP is an Integer.

83
00:04:24.212 --> 00:04:27.949
To store 12 in it, the code calls Integer.valueOf.

84
00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:32.508
A method call isn't a constant, so reading CAP is a real use.

85
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:38.279
A constant is like a line the director photocopied into everyone's script.

86
00:04:38.279 --> 00:04:41.553
Nobody phones the props table to find out it's 12.

87
00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:45.670
So reading a constant isn't a use of its class at all.

88
00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:46.537
It's a copy.

89
00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:49.268
Kotlin draws the same line.

90
00:04:49.268 --> 00:04:51.469
const val is copied into callers.

91
00:04:51.469 --> 00:04:55.871
A plain val is read through the object, so it runs the init block.

92
00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.290
Now the names fit things you've seen.

93
00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:00.366
Loading fetches.

94
00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.057
Linking verifies, prepares and resolves.

95
00:05:03.057 --> 00:05:06.960
Initialization runs &lt;clinit&gt;, once, on the first real use.

96
00:05:07.867 --> 00:05:09.519
Two rules to take with you.

97
00:05:09.519 --> 00:05:11.967
One: mentioning a class is not using it.

98
00:05:11.967 --> 00:05:16.740
If you need its start-up code to have run, check that your use is on the list.

99
00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:24.295
Two: turn a constant into a computed value, like a number read from a setting, and you change when the class starts up.

100
00:05:24.295 --> 00:05:26.943
That's the day the rehearsal quietly moves.

101
00:05:27.867 --> 00:05:30.498
So, does mentioning a class wake it up?

102
00:05:30.498 --> 00:05:30.700
No.

103
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.602
Fetch, then check and label, then rehearse.

104
00:05:33.602 --> 00:05:37.920
Only the rehearsal runs your code, and only a real use calls it.

105
00:05:38.133 --> 00:05:39.188
Quick check.

106
00:05:39.188 --> 00:05:41.738
Some code runs new Limits[3].

107
00:05:41.738 --> 00:05:43.145
Does init print?

108
00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:46.368
No.

109
00:05:46.368 --> 00:05:50.987
An empty array holds no Limits yet, so the rehearsal waits.

110
00:05:50.987 --> 00:05:52.788
Step 5 printed nothing.
